<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fascist Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kingdom of Italy under Fascism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fascist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, from his appointment as prime minister in 1922 to his fall in 1943. The regime suppressed opposition, reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935 and aided Franco in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. It joined Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, which brought its collapse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fascist-italy.jpg" alt="Fascist Italy" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keystone-France/Gamma-Keystone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_1930.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> from 1922 to 1943, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> as prime minister turning the country into a totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. The regime crushed political opposition, while promoting economic modernisation, traditional social values and a rapprochement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. The historian Stanley G. Payne distinguishes six phases. The first, from 1922 to 1925, was nominally a continuation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>, though with a legally organised executive dictatorship, and the second, from 1925 to 1929, was the construction of the Fascist dictatorship proper. In 1929 Mussolini and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> reached an agreement that ended a standoff between the Church and the Italian state going back to 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its first years the regime pacified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, bombed and occupied Corfu in 1923, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1925. The fourth phase, from 1935 to 1940, was marked by an aggressive foreign policy: the Second Italo-Ethiopian War, launched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> sanctions, growing economic autarky, the invasion of Albania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-steel">Pact of Steel</a>. In 1938 the regime passed racial laws that stripped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of their Italian citizenship, barred them from professional positions and forbade marriages between Italians and Jews or Africans. Mussolini had until then denied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo">Italo Balbo</a> opposed the laws.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was a leading member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and fought at first with some success. After the German-Italian defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Allied landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> overthrew and arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, and the new government signed an armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in September 1943. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> seized the north and rescued Mussolini, setting up the Italian Social Republic, a puppet state, and Italian resistance groups fought German and Republic forces until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The regime set out to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, and the Doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> of 1935 declared a totalitarian state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> created the Blackshirt militia in 1923 and the secret police <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovra">OVRA</a> in 1927, which terrorised opponents, in some cases with forced doses of castor oil. The state took control of textbooks in 1929, and in 1933 university teachers had to belong to the party.</p><p>The Opera Nazionale Dopolavoro, founded in 1925, organised leisure for adults and by 1936 had reached an estimated 80 per cent of salaried workers. The Fascists promised corporatism, with unions and employers grouped into professional corporations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> was captured and killed by the resistance on 28 April 1945. In 1946 Italians voted in a referendum to abandon the monarchy and form the Italian Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fascist Italy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new government was a one-party dictatorship explicitly modelled on <b>Fascist Italy</b> and Nazi Germany, with its own Spanish characteristics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy was subordinated to Nazi Germany and <b>Fascist Italy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fascist Italy</b>, Nazi Germany and Portugal gave the rebels much greater and more effective help than the Soviet Union, Mexico and the International Brigades gave the Republic, while France and Britain kept to non-intervention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Fascist Italy</b> and Nazi Germany destroyed the independent unions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Totalitarian regimes, a term first used of <b>Fascist Italy</b> in the 1920s, are dictatorships that use a single party, an official ideology, terror and control of the economy and mass media to try to remake society itself, not merely to rule it.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In Libya <b>Fascist Italy</b> waged a campaign of mass killings, concentration camps and the forced starvation of thousands, and expelled 100,000 Bedouin Cyrenaicans, half the population of Cyrenaica.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
